 At this point, I'd like to take a little bit of a step back and look at how two mechanical properties vary with temperature across families and then talk about how

6
00:00:29,280 --> 00:00:39,440
 ad hoc documents, some of this information. I think it should be reasonably intuitive to at this point that most materials see a substantial decrease in some of their mechanical properties as temperature is increased.

7
00:00:39,440 --> 00:00:46,080
 For example, the strength of many materials decreases as a function of temperature, many materials soften as they're heated.

8
00:00:46,080 --> 00:00:51,200
 And it's probably not a bad idea for us to try and draw some links now between the different families of materials.

9
00:00:51,280 --> 00:00:59,520
 Metals, for example, soften considerably when they're heated because the extra vibration in the lattice essentially makes it easier for dislocations to move.

10
00:00:59,520 --> 00:01:04,320
 If dislocations can move more easily, then the effective strength of the material is lessened.

11
00:01:04,320 --> 00:01:12,560
 In polymers, softening with temperature was largely a function of weakening of the already not particularly great van der Waals bonds in between chains.

12
00:01:12,560 --> 00:01:16,960
 And you'll probably remember that this effect was somewhat lessened for polymers that were very heavily crosslinked.

13
00:01:17,040 --> 00:01:24,240
 Polymers like thermosets that don't necessarily rely only on van der Waals bonds, but have some covalent bonds in between chains as well.

14
00:01:24,240 --> 00:01:31,920
 And I hope you'll remember that this week we talked about how most engineering ceramics don't really see an appreciable loss in strength with increasing temperature.

15
00:01:31,920 --> 00:01:41,680
 This is largely because in engineering ceramics that ionic and covalent bonds that make them up so strong and the lattices are so rigid that dislocations can't really move anyway.

16
00:01:41,680 --> 00:01:52,080
 Any small amount of softening that we do see with temperature in engineering ceramics is largely related to weakening of the covalent ionic bonds, but because they're so strong to begin with, this effect is pretty small.

17
00:01:52,080 --> 00:02:04,160
 Whereas the mechanisms for decreasing strength with temperature increase are largely material family specific, the mechanism governing a decrease in stiffness as a function of temperature is pretty much the same across different families of materials.

18
00:02:04,160 --> 00:02:09,520
 In all cases, it's an effective weakening of the atomic bonds between atoms that leads to a decrease in stiffness.

19
00:02:09,520 --> 00:02:20,000
 In metals, it's a weakening of the metallic bonds. In polymers, it's largely a weakening of the van der Waals bonds between chains or for heavily crosslinked polymers, some amount of weakening of the covalent bonds between atoms.

20
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:27,280
 And for ceramics, as was the case with strengths, though the effect is very small, it's due to weakening of the covalent and the ionic bonds that make them up.

21
00:02:27,360 --> 00:02:33,360
 For stiffness, particularly, there are many models that attempt to capture the decrease in youngs modulus with increasing temperature.

22
00:02:33,360 --> 00:02:36,800
 This is a relatively simple linear one that you may choose to use.

23
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:43,200
 And while this model is relatively good for some metals, it doesn't hold particularly true for ceramics or for polymers.

24
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:51,360
 I've left some resources on the textbook page for this video that dove into a little bit more detail as to why that is and what models might be more appropriate there.

